<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-4-8-newtons-method" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Newton's Method</title>
  <!-- Stewart 4.8 . Skill 5 . No handout exists for this section; written 2026-09-11
       in the same conversational voice. Running example x^3 - 2x - 5 = 0 from x1 = 2. -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-newton">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        Some equations have no formula for their solution. How do we solve them anyway?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      You can solve <m>x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0</m>. Factor it, or use the quadratic formula.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now try <m>x^3 - 2x - 5 = 0</m>. Or <m>\cos x = x</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the first one, a formula does exist, but it is so messy that almost nobody uses it.
      For the second one, there is no formula at all.
      Yet both equations have a solution, and a calculator can find it in a blink.
      Let's see how.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-8-lines-are-easy">
    <title>Curves are hard, lines are easy</title>

    <p>
      Here is the one idea behind this whole section.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finding where a <em>curve</em> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis is hard.
      Finding where a <em>line</em> crosses it is easy.
    </p>
    <p>
      And near any point, a smooth curve looks almost exactly like its tangent line.
      Zoom in far enough, and the curve straightens out.
    </p>
    <p>
      So here is the plan. Replace the curve with its tangent line.
      Find where the <em>line</em> crosses the axis. Use that as a better guess. Repeat.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-line-crossing" label="check-line-crossing">
      <title>Warm-up: where does a line cross?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A line passes through the point <m>(2, -1)</m> with slope <m>10</m>.
          Where does it cross the <m>x</m>-axis?
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>x =</m> <fillin answer="2.1" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.0001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The line has to rise <m>1</m> unit to reach the axis. With slope <m>10</m>, that takes a run of <m>\tfrac{1}{10} = 0.1</m>. So <m>x = 2 + 0.1 = 2.1</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Hold onto that <m>2.1</m>. It is about to matter.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-8-one-step">
    <title>One step at a time</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>f(x) = x^3 - 2x - 5</m>. We want to solve <m>f(x) = 0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Make a first guess.</alert> <m>f(2) = -1</m> and <m>f(3) = 16</m>.
      The output changes sign between <m>2</m> and <m>3</m>, so a solution lives in there.
      And <m>-1</m> is much closer to <m>0</m> than <m>16</m> is.
      So start with <m>x_1 = 2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Draw the tangent line at <m>x_1</m>.</alert> A line needs a point and a slope.
      The point is <m>(2, f(2)) = (2, -1)</m>.
      The slope is <m>f'(2)</m>. Since <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 2</m>, the slope is <m>f'(2) = 10</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is exactly the line from the warm-up! It crosses the axis at <m>x_2 = 2.1</m>.
      That is our new guess.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Is it better?</alert> <m>f(2.1) = 0.061</m>. Much closer to <m>0</m> than <m>-1</m> was.
    </p>
    <p>
      So do it again, starting from <m>2.1</m>.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-newton-step-two" label="check-newton-step-two">
      <title>You do: take the next step</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep going with <m>f(x) = x^3 - 2x - 5</m> and <m>x_2 = 2.1</m>.
          You already know <m>f(2.1) = 0.061</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Slope of the tangent line: <m>f'(2.1) =</m> <fillin answer="11.23" mode="number"/>
        </p>
        <p>
          Where that line crosses the axis: <m>x_3 \approx</m> <fillin answer="2.0946" mode="number"/>
          (four decimal places)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>3(2.1)^2 - 2 = 3(4.41) - 2 = 11.23</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.0001"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. The line drops <m>0.061</m> at slope <m>11.23</m>, so it moves left by <m>\tfrac{0.061}{11.23} \approx 0.0054</m>. That gives <m>2.1 - 0.0054 \approx 2.0946</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Look how fast that went: <m>x_1 = 2</m>, <m>x_2 = 2.1</m>, <m>x_3 \approx 2.0946</m>.
      The true solution is <m>2.09455\ldots</m>
      Two steps, and the first four decimal places already match.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-newton-steps">
      <caption>Each colored segment is a tangent line. Where it hits the axis is the next guess.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-newton-steps" label="interactive-newton-steps"
                   platform="jsxgraph" width="100%" aspect="4:3"
                   source="jsxgraph/newton-steps.js">
        <slate xml:id="interactive-newton-steps-slate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
        <instructions>
          <p>
            The blue curve is <m>y = x^3 - 2x - 5</m>. Drag the red point <m>x_1</m>
            along the axis to pick a first guess, and watch three steps of the method.
            Try a starting guess close to <m>0.8</m>. What goes wrong?
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-8-formula">
    <title>Now give the idea a name</title>

    <p>
      Let's do one step with letters instead of numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tangent line at <m>x_n</m> goes through the point <m>(x_n, f(x_n))</m>
      with slope <m>f'(x_n)</m>:
      <md>
        y = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n).
      </md>
      (That is the tangent line approximation, or linearization, from Calculus I.)
    </p>
    <p>
      We want where it crosses the axis. So set <m>y = 0</m> and solve for <m>x</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> 0 &amp;= f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) </mrow>
        <mrow> x - x_n &amp;= -\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)} </mrow>
        <mrow> x &amp;= x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}. </mrow>
      </md>
      That <m>x</m> is our next guess, <m>x_{n+1}</m>.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-newtons-method">
      <title>Newton's Method</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          To solve <m>f(x) = 0</m>, start with a guess <m>x_1</m>. Then repeat
          <md>
            x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}
          </md>
          until the guesses stop changing, to the accuracy you need.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Say it out loud</title>
      <p>New guess equals old guess, minus height over slope.</p>
      <p>
        The height <m>f(x_n)</m> says how far you are from zero.
        The slope <m>f'(x_n)</m> says how fast you are heading there.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise xml:id="check-newton-cosine" label="check-newton-cosine">
      <title>Less scaffolding: where does cos x = x?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\cos x = x</m> with Newton's method. Use radians.
        </p>
        <p>
          First rewrite it as <m>f(x) = 0</m> with <m>f(x) = \cos x - x</m>.
          Then <m>f'(x) = -\sin x - 1</m>. Start at <m>x_1 = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>x_2 \approx</m> <fillin answer="0.7504" mode="number"/>
          &#160;&#160;&#160; <m>x_3 \approx</m> <fillin answer="0.7391" mode="number"/>
          &#160;(four decimal places)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.0002"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>f(1) \approx -0.4597</m> and <m>f'(1) \approx -1.8415</m>, so <m>x_2 \approx 1 - \dfrac{-0.4597}{-1.8415} \approx 0.7504</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.0002"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. One more step lands on <m>0.7391</m>. The next step barely moves it, so we have found the solution to four decimal places.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-4-8-what-goes-wrong">
    <title>What could go wrong?</title>

    <p>
      Newton's method is fast when it works. But it can fail.
      Think about the picture before you open the solution.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="notice-newton-flat-tangent">
      <title>A flat tangent line</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose your guess lands where <m>f'(x_n) = 0</m>. Sketch the tangent line there.
          What happens to the next guess?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A flat tangent line never crosses the <m>x</m>-axis (unless you were already on it).
          In the formula, you would divide by <m>0</m>. There is no next guess.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even a <em>nearly</em> flat tangent is trouble. It crosses the axis very far away,
          and the next guess can land nowhere near the solution. That is what you saw when
          you started the interactive near <m>0.8</m>, where <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 2</m> is close to <m>0</m>.
          The fix: pick a better first guess.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>A good first guess matters. Check where <m>f</m> changes sign, and start close.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise xml:id="exit-ticket-newton">
      <title>Exit ticket</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>In your own words, what does one step of Newton's method do?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why do we use the tangent line instead of the curve?</p></li>
            <li><p>Give one way Newton's method can fail.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>It replaces the curve with its tangent line at the current guess, and uses the spot where that line crosses the axis as the next guess.</p></li>
            <li><p>We cannot solve for where the curve crosses, but we can always solve for where a line crosses. And near the guess, the line is a good stand-in for the curve.</p></li>
            <li><p>If <m>f'(x_n) = 0</m>, the tangent line is flat and never crosses the axis. A bad first guess can also send the guesses far away.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Main idea</title>
      <p>
        When we cannot solve an equation, we solve an easier one instead: the tangent line.
        Repeat, and the answers close in on the real solution, fast.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>
</section>
